## 1. Creating a Tuple

A tuple is created by placing elements inside parentheses `()`.

```python
languages = ("Python", "Java", "C++")

print(languages)
```

A tuple can also contain different data types:

```python
student = ("Vihari", 21, 8.5, True)

print(student)
```

---

## 2. Tuple Indexing

Tuple indexing starts from `0`.

```python
languages = ("Python", "Java", "C++")

print(languages[0])
print(languages[1])
print(languages[-1])
```

**Output:**

```text
Python
Java
C++
```

---

## 3. Tuple Slicing

Tuples support slicing just like strings and lists.

```python
numbers = (10, 20, 30, 40, 50)

print(numbers[1:4])
print(numbers[:3])
print(numbers[2:])
```

---

## 4. Tuples are Immutable

Tuples cannot be modified after they are created.

```python
numbers = (10, 20, 30)

# This will cause an error
# numbers[0] = 100
```

This makes tuples useful when data should remain unchanged.

---

## 5. Single-Element Tuple

A comma is required when creating a tuple with only one element.

```python
single = (10,)

print(type(single))
```

Without the comma:

```python
single = (10)

print(type(single))
```

This is treated as an integer, not a tuple.

---

## 6. Tuple Packing

Packing means placing multiple values into a tuple.

```python
student = "Vihari", 21, "AIML"

print(student)
```

---

## 7. Tuple Unpacking

Unpacking assigns tuple elements to separate variables.

```python
student = ("Vihari", 21, "AIML")

name, age, branch = student

print(name)
print(age)
print(branch)
```

---

## 8. Nested Tuples

A tuple can contain another tuple.

```python
data = ("Vihari", (85, 90, 88))

print(data[0])
print(data[1])
print(data[1][0])
```

---

## 9. Tuple Methods

Tuples have two main built-in methods.

### `count()`

Counts how many times a value appears.

```python
numbers = (10, 20, 10, 30, 10)

print(numbers.count(10))
```

### `index()`

Returns the position of a value.

```python
numbers = (10, 20, 30, 40)

print(numbers.index(30))
```

---

## 10. Useful Tuple Operations

```python
numbers = (10, 20, 30, 40)

print(len(numbers))
print(max(numbers))
print(min(numbers))
print(sum(numbers))
print(20 in numbers)
```

---

## 🔄 Tuple vs List

| Feature    | List            | Tuple      |
| ---------- | --------------- | ---------- |
| Syntax     | `[]`            | `()`       |
| Mutable    | Yes             | No         |
| Ordered    | Yes             | Yes        |
| Duplicates | Allowed         | Allowed    |
| Methods    | More            | Fewer      |
| Best for   | Changeable data | Fixed data |

